  "worked_solution": "We're given the **rate of change** of volume $r(t).$ To find the **net change** in volume between the times $t=1$ and $t=3,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}20{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={-\\frac{20}{2}{e}^{-2t}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & ={-10{e}^{-2t}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-10({e}^{-6}-{e}^{-2}) \\\\  & \u22481.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place. \n\nTherefore, between $t=1$ minute and $t=3$ minutes, $1.3$ liters of water entered the tank.",
